  "completedStatus": "Completed",
  "hivHeroDesc": "HIV/AIDS is a virus that weakens the immune system, spreads mainly through unsafe sex, blood, and mother-to-child, but can be prevented with safe practices and managed effectively with early testing and treatment (ART).",
  "hivNoteTitle": "Note",
  "hivNoteContent": "HIV can be prevented and managed—practice safe behaviors, get tested early, and start treatment (ART) to live a healthy life and protect others.",
  "stiHeroDesc": "STIs are infections spread mainly through unprotected sexual contact; they may show no symptoms but can cause serious health complications if untreated, and can be managed effectively with early diagnosis and proper treatment.",
  "stiNoteContent": "Protect yourself by practicing safe sex, getting tested regularly, and seeking medical care early knowing your status helps prevent complications and protects others.",
  "stiDefinitionTitle": "Definition",
  "stiDefinitionSubtitle": "What are Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs)?",
  "stiDefinitionDesc1": "Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) are infections passed from person to person primarily through sexual contact (vaginal, oral, or anal intercourse). They are caused by bacteria, viruses, or parasites.",
  "stiDefinitionDesc2": "Common bacterial STIs include Chlamydia, Gonorrhea, and Syphilis. Common viral STIs include HPV (Human Papillomavirus), Herpes (HSV), Hepatitis B, and HIV. Common parasitic STIs include Trichomoniasis.",
  "stiDefinitionDesc3": "An infection (STI) means a pathogen is present in the body, often without causing symptoms. It becomes a disease (STD) when the infection causes noticeable damage or illness. Many STIs are silent but still contagious.",
  "stiSymptomsTitle": "Signs and Symptoms of STI",
  "stiSymptomsSubtitle": "Recognizing common signs and asymptomatic cases",
  "stiSymptomsIntro": "Symptoms can vary depending on the specific infection and gender, but commonly include:",
  "stiSymptom1": "Unusual discharge: Thick, watery, yellow, green, or foul-smelling discharge from the penis, vagina, or anus.",
  "stiSymptom2": "Pain or burning sensation: Discomfort or burning pain during urination (dysuria) or during sexual intercourse.",
  "stiSymptom3": "Sores, bumps, or blisters: Painful or painless blisters, ulcers, warts, or lesions on the genitals, groin, mouth, or anus.",
  "stiSymptom4": "Itching and irritation: Persistent itching, redness, or swelling around the genital and perineal areas.",
  "stiSymptom5": "Abdominal or pelvic pain: Lower belly pain, unexpected bleeding between menstrual periods, or bleeding after intercourse.",
  "stiSymptomsAsymptomaticAlert": "Silent infections: Up to 70% of women and many men with STIs like chlamydia or gonorrhea experience NO noticeable symptoms. Regular clinical testing is the only reliable way to confirm status.",
  "stiDiagnosisTitle": "Diagnosis of STI",
  "stiDiagnosisSubtitle": "How healthcare providers detect and evaluate STIs",
  "stiDiagnosisIntro": "Early diagnosis ensures quick cure and prevents irreversible reproductive damage. Healthcare providers use:",
  "stiDiagnosisItem1": "Clinical examination: Confidential, private, and respectful physical assessment by a trained health professional.",
  "stiDiagnosisItem2": "Laboratory tests: Blood tests (for Syphilis, HIV, Hepatitis B), urine samples, and swabs from fluid or sores.",
  "stiDiagnosisItem3": "National syndromic management: In primary health clinics across Ethiopia, healthcare workers follow national guidelines to treat symptoms immediately on the first visit without waiting days for lab results.",
  "stiDiagnosisYouthFriendly": "Youth-friendly centers provide completely confidential, stigma-free counseling, tests, and care for adolescents and youth.",
  "stiComplicationsTitle": "Complications of STI",
  "stiComplicationsSubtitle": "Serious risks of leaving STIs untreated",
  "stiComplicationsIntro": "When left untreated or improperly treated, STIs can lead to severe and lasting health consequences:",
  "stiComplication1": "Infertility & PID: Untreated chlamydia or gonorrhea can cause Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) in women, leading to scarring of fallopian tubes, chronic pain, life-threatening ectopic pregnancy, or infertility. In men, it can cause painful epididymitis and infertility.",
  "stiComplication2": "Higher HIV risk: Open genital sores and mucosal inflammation from STIs multiply the risk of contracting or transmitting HIV by 3 to 5 times.",
  "stiComplication3": "Mother-to-child transmission: Pregnant mothers with untreated STIs can pass infections to their babies, leading to miscarriage, stillbirth, premature birth, low birth weight, and newborn eye infections.",
  "stiComplication4": "Cervical & other cancers: Persistent infections with high-risk HPV strains are the primary cause of cervical cancer in women.",
  "stiTreatmentTitle": "Management and treatment of STI",
  "stiTreatmentSubtitle": "Medical therapies and critical rules for recovery",
  "stiTreatmentDesc1": "Bacterial and parasitic STIs (Chlamydia, Gonorrhea, Syphilis, Trichomoniasis) are fully curable with specific prescription antibiotics or antiparasitics.",
  "stiTreatmentDesc2": "Viral STIs (Herpes, HPV, HIV, Hepatitis B) cannot be cured with antibiotics, but modern antiviral medications effectively manage symptoms, reduce recurrences, and prevent complications.",
  "stiTreatmentRule1": "Finish all medication: Always complete the full prescribed course, even if symptoms vanish early. Incomplete treatment creates antibiotic-resistant strains.",
  "stiTreatmentRule2": "Treat sexual partners simultaneously: Both partners must receive treatment at the same time to prevent passing the infection back and forth (ping-pong effect).",
  "stiTreatmentRule3": "Abstain during treatment: Refrain from all sexual intercourse until both partners finish all medicines and clinical symptoms are completely gone.",
  "stiTreatmentRule4": "Never self-medicate: Do not take leftover pills or buy random antibiotics from pharmacies without a medical diagnosis, as wrong medications can make infections worse.",
  "stiPreventionTitle": "Prevention of STI",
  "stiPreventionSubtitle": "Effective ways to protect yourself and your partner",
  "stiPreventionIntro": "Protecting yourself from STIs requires practical, consistent safe behaviors:",
  "stiPreventionItem1": "Consistent & correct condom use: Latex and polyurethane male and female condoms provide highly effective barrier protection against most STIs when used properly every single time.",
  "stiPreventionItem2": "Mutual fidelity: Being in a faithful relationship with one uninfected partner after both have been tested.",
  "stiPreventionItem3": "Abstinence: Choosing not to engage in sexual intercourse is the only 100% foolproof way to prevent STIs.",
  "stiPreventionItem4": "Vaccination: Vaccines against HPV (protects against cervical cancer) and Hepatitis B are safe, highly effective, and available.",
  "stiPreventionItem5": "Regular checkups & screening: Getting tested before starting a new sexual relationship and getting screened annually if sexually active.",
  "stiPreventionItem6": "Avoid alcohol and substance abuse: Intoxication impairs judgment and significantly reduces the use of protection.",
  "hepatitisHeroDesc": "Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver caused by viruses or other factors, often showing mild or no symptoms but potentially leading to serious complications like cirrhosis, liver failure, or cancer if untreated; early diagnosis and proper management are essential.",
  "hepatitisNoteContent": "Hepatitis can be prevented and managed—practice good hygiene, avoid risky behaviors, get vaccinated where possible, and seek early testing to protect your liver health.",
  "hepatitisSection1Title": "Definition",
  "hepatitisSection1Desc1": "Hepatitis is a general term used to describe inflammation of the liver. Liver inflammation can be caused by several viruses (viral hepatitis), chemicals, drugs, alcohol, certain genetic disorders or by an overactive immune system that mistakenly attacks the liver, called autoimmune hepatitis.",
  "hepatitisSection1Desc2": "There are several types of hepatitis, which are categorized as viral hepatitis and non-viral hepatitis. The most common types of viral hepatitis are A, B, C, D, and E. Then there are non-viral causes of hepatitis, such as autoimmune hepatitis, alcoholic hepatitis, and drug-induced hepatitis.",
  "hepatitisSection2Title": "Sign and symptoms of Hepatitis",
  "hepatitisSection2Intro": "Many people with hepatitis A, B, C, D or E exhibit only mild symptoms or no symptoms at all. Each form of the virus, however, can cause more severe symptoms. Symptoms of hepatitis A, B and C may include: -",
  "hepatitisSymptom1": "fever",
  "hepatitisSymptom2": "malaise",
  "hepatitisSymptom3": "loss of appetite",
  "hepatitisSymptom4": "diarrhea",
  "hepatitisSymptom5": "nausea",
  "hepatitisSymptom6": "abdominal discomfort",
  "hepatitisSymptom7": "dark-colored urine and jaundice (a yellowing of the skin and whites of the eyes).",
  "hepatitisSection2ChronicNote": "In some cases, the virus can also cause a chronic liver infection that can later develop into cirrhosis (a scarring of the liver) or liver cancer. These patients are at risk of death.",
  "hepatitisBTitle": "Hepatitis B",
  "hepatitisBDesc": "Hepatitis B is a viral infection that affects the liver, caused by the Hepatitis B virus (HBV). It can range from a mild illness to a serious, long-term (chronic) condition that can lead to liver cirrhosis, liver failure, or liver cancer.",
  "hepatitisBTransmissionIntro": "The virus is transmitted through contact with infectious body fluids, such as blood, semen, and vaginal fluids, commonly through:",
  "hepatitisBTransmission1": "Unprotected sexual contact",
  "hepatitisBTransmission2": "Sharing needles or other drug paraphernalia",
  "hepatitisBTransmission3": "From an infected mother to her newborn during childbirth",
  "hepatitisBTransmission4": "Exposure to contaminated blood in healthcare settings",
  "hepatitisBAlert": "Hepatitis B is preventable with a vaccine, but it has no cure.",
  "hepatitisCTitle": "Hepatitis C",
  "hepatitisCDesc": "Hepatitis C is a viral infection caused by the Hepatitis C virus (HCV), primarily affecting the liver. It is most commonly spread through blood-to-blood contact, often through:",
  "hepatitisCTransmission1": "Sharing needles or other drug equipment",
  "hepatitisCTransmission2": "Receiving contaminated blood products or unscreened transfusions",
  "hepatitisCTransmission3": "Mother-to-child transmission during childbirth (less common than Hepatitis B)",
  "hepatitisCTransmission4": "Unprotected sexual practices that involve potential blood contact",
  "hepatitisCAlert": "Unlike Hepatitis B, there is currently no vaccine for Hepatitis C, but over 95% of cases can be cured with direct-acting antiviral (DAA) medications.",
  "hepatitisSection3Title": "Diagnosis of Hepatitis",
  "hepatitisSection3Intro": "Because viral hepatitis often shows mild or no early symptoms, laboratory diagnostic screening is critical for early detection and liver protection:",
  "hepatitisDiagnosis1": "Serological blood tests: Testing for HBV surface antigen (HBsAg) or HCV antibodies.",
  "hepatitisDiagnosis2": "Liver function tests (LFTs): Measuring liver enzymes (ALT, AST, bilirubin) to evaluate liver inflammation and function.",
  "hepatitisDiagnosis3": "Viral load molecular tests: PCR tests detecting viral RNA/DNA to confirm active infection and monitor treatment effectiveness.",
  "hepatitisDiagnosis4": "Liver imaging & elastography: Ultrasound scans and FibroScan to evaluate liver stiffness, scarring, and check for cirrhosis.",
  "hepatitisSection4Title": "Complication of Hepatitis",
  "hepatitisSection4Intro": "Without timely medical monitoring and management, chronic hepatitis progression can lead to severe, life-threatening complications:",
  "hepatitisComplication1": "Liver Cirrhosis: Progressive fibrotic scarring of liver tissue that permanently replaces healthy functional cells.",
  "hepatitisComplication2": "Liver Failure (End-Stage Liver Disease): Complete inability of the liver to filter toxins, produce clotting proteins, and regulate metabolism.",
  "hepatitisComplication3": "Hepatocellular Carcinoma (Liver Cancer): Chronic Hepatitis B and C infections are leading causes of primary liver cancer worldwide.",
  "hepatitisComplication4": "Portal Hypertension & Ascites: Elevated blood pressure in the portal vein causing fluid buildup in the abdomen and bleeding varices.",
  "hepatitisSection5Title": "Management and treatment of Hepatitis",
  "hepatitisSection5Intro": "Clinical management depends on the specific viral type and whether the infection is acute or chronic:",
  "hepatitisTreatment1": "Chronic Hepatitis B: Lifelong oral antiviral medications (such as Tenofovir or Entecavir) that suppress viral replication, halt liver damage, and prevent cancer.",
  "hepatitisTreatment2": "Chronic Hepatitis C: Highly effective 8–12 week oral direct-acting antiviral (DAA) therapy achieving cure rates above 95%.",
  "hepatitisTreatment3": "Lifestyle modifications: Complete avoidance of alcohol, balanced liver-friendly nutrition, avoiding hepatotoxic herbal compounds, and maintaining a healthy weight.",
  "hepatitisTreatment4": "Routine clinical monitoring: Regular liver function tests, viral load assessments, and ultrasound cancer surveillance.",
  "hepatitisSection6Title": "Prevention Of Hepatitis",
  "hepatitisSection6Intro": "Hepatitis transmission can be effectively interrupted through proactive vaccination, barrier hygiene, and clinical safety measures:",
  "hepatitisPrevention1": "Vaccination: Timely Hepatitis B vaccination (especially the birth dose within 24 hours of delivery, followed by infant series) and Hepatitis A vaccination.",
  "hepatitisPrevention2": "Safe injection practices: Using sterile single-use needles and avoiding sharing any injection or body-piercing equipment.",
  "hepatitisPrevention3": "Safer sexual practices: Consistent and correct condom use to prevent Hepatitis B and co-infection with other STIs or HIV.",
  "hepatitisPrevention4": "Clean water and food hygiene: Washing hands thoroughly with soap, drinking safe treated water, and hygienic food prep to prevent Hepatitis A and E.",
  "hepatitisPrevention5": "Blood safety: Rigorous screening of all donated blood products and stringent sterilization of medical/dental instruments.",
  "substanceHeroDesc": "Substance abuse is the harmful use of drugs or alcohol that affects health, behavior, and relationships, leading to physical and mental problems, dependency, and serious life consequences if not addressed early.",
  "substanceNoteContent": "Substance abuse is preventable and treatable—recognizing early signs and seeking help can protect your health, relationships, and future.",
  "substanceDefTitle": "Definition of substance abuse",
  "substanceDefDesc1": "Substance abuse refers to the harmful or hazardous use of psychoactive substances, including alcohol and illicit drugs, characterized by a compulsive pattern of consumption. This behavior often leads to significant adverse consequences in various aspects of an individual's life, such as health, relationships, and social responsibilities. It can result in physical and psychological dependence, tolerance, and withdrawal symptoms.",
  "substanceDefDesc2": "Psychoactive substance is a drug or other substance that affects how the brain works and causes changes in mood, awareness, thoughts, feelings, or behavior. The commonly used psychoactive substances include alcohol, caffeine, nicotine, marijuana, and certain pain medicines.",
  "substanceDefDesc3": "Substance use disorder (SUD) is a mental disorder that affects a person's brain and behavior, leading to a person's inability to control their use of substances such as legal or illegal drugs, alcohol, or medications. A substance use disorder involves using too much alcohol, tobacco or other drugs.",
  "substanceTypesTitle": "Types of drugs/Substances",
  "substanceTypeDepressants": "Depressants: - the most commonly found types of drugs in society are depressants. Alcohol, Sedatives/hypnotics.",
  "substanceTypeStimulants": "Stimulants: - Increase energy, alertness, and wakefulness. Such as Nicotine, Cocaine, Amphetamines and Caffeine.",
  "substanceTypeOpioids": "Opioids: - These are pain-killing drugs that increase feelings of happiness or euphoria and create a tranquilizing effect. Heroin, morphine, opium, buprenorphine, methadone, pethidine and Cough syrup with codeine.",
  "substanceTypeHallucinogens": "Hallucinogens: - They can also cause a person to perceive time differently, feel detached from their surroundings, or feel deeply insightful.",
  "substanceEffectsTitle": "Effects of substance abuse",
  "substanceEffectsIntro": "Substance abuse refers to a pattern of using drugs or alcohol that leads to significant problems or distress in an individual's life. This can manifest in various ways, including:",
  "substanceEffect1": "Increased risk of disease (Liver diseases, heart diseases, respiratory problems, HIV)",
  "substanceEffect2": "Mental health disorder increased risk of anxiety, depression etc.",
  "substanceEffect3": "Missing work or school due to substance use.",
  "substanceEffect4": "Engaging in dangerous activities while under the influence, such as driving.",
  "substanceEffect5": "Experiencing legal issues related to substance use.",
  "substanceEffect6": "Strained relationships with family and friends due to ongoing substance use.",
  "substanceEffect7": "Substance abuse can involve both illegal drugs (like heroin, cocaine, and methamphetamine) and the misuse of prescription drugs or legal substances.",
  "substanceSignsTitle": "Signs of a Substance Use Problem",
  "substanceSignsIntro": "Recognizing early behavioral and physical indicators helps individuals get timely care before severe complications arise:",
  "substanceSign1": "Compulsive cravings: Intense urges to consume the substance daily or multiple times a day.",
  "substanceSign2": "Developing tolerance: Needing larger amounts over time to produce the initial desired effect.",
  "substanceSign3": "Withdrawal symptoms: Experiencing restlessness, tremors, sweating, anxiety, or nausea when attempting to stop.",
  "substanceSign4": "Neglecting responsibilities: Falling behind in school performance, missing work shifts, or ignoring family duties.",
  "substanceSign5": "Abandoning interests: Giving up sports, creative hobbies, and social activities once enjoyed.",
  "substanceSign6": "Social withdrawal and secretive behavior: Withdrawing from family gatherings, hiding substance use, or drastic personality shifts.",
  "substanceSignsHelpAlert": "Substance use disorder is a treatable health condition, not a sign of moral weakness. Youth mentors, counseling facilities, and national toll-free hotlines (952 or 8335) provide confidential, compassionate assistance.",
  "substanceQuizTitle": "Substance Abuse Quiz",
  "substanceQ1Text": "Is substance use disorder (SUD) a treatable medical condition affecting the brain and behavior, rather than a failure of willpower or personal morality?",
  "substanceQ1Insight": "Substance use disorders alter brain chemistry and neural reward pathways. Recognized by medical authorities worldwide, recovery is achievable through evidence-based treatment, counseling, and social support.",
  "substanceQ2Text": "Which category of substances slows down central nervous system function, heart rate, and brain activity (such as alcohol and sedatives)?",
  "substanceQ2OptA": "Depressants",
  "substanceQ2OptB": "Stimulants",
  "substanceQ2OptC": "Hallucinogens",
  "substanceQ2OptD": "Vitamins",
  "substanceQ2Insight": "Depressants slow down neural messaging and heart rate, leading to reduced alertness and relaxation. In contrast, stimulants speed up the nervous system.",
  "substanceQ3Text": "What does developing \"tolerance\" mean when someone regularly consumes drugs or alcohol?",
  "substanceQ3OptA": "The body needs increasingly larger amounts over time to produce the initial desired effect",
  "substanceQ3OptB": "The substance permanently leaves the body after a single use",
  "substanceQ3OptC": "The person can immediately stop taking the substance with zero symptoms",
  "substanceQ3OptD": "Having a complete natural immunity to all chemicals",
  "substanceQ3Insight": "Tolerance occurs when cells and brain receptors adapt to regular substance exposure, leading people to consume higher doses to feel the original impact.",
  "substanceQ4Text": "Substance abuse is both preventable and treatable, and free national health hotlines (952 or 8335) provide confidential, judgment-free support?",
  "substanceQ4Insight": "Early intervention saves lives. Confidential health hotlines (952 or 8335) connect youth with empathetic professionals, referrals to youth-friendly centers, and recovery guidance.",
  "mentalHealthHeroDesc": "Mental health is an essential part of overall health, encompassing emotional, psychological, and social wellbeing. It influences how we think, feel, manage stress, connect with others, and make decisions throughout life.",
  "mentalHealthNoteContent": "Mental health exists on a continuum and can be nurtured and healed—seeking support during tough times is a sign of courage and strength, not weakness.",
  "mentalHealthSection1Title": "Understanding Mental Health & Wellbeing",
  "mentalHealthSection1Desc1": "Mental health includes our emotional, psychological, and social wellbeing. It affects how we handle daily challenges, relate to friends and family, and make healthy lifestyle choices.",
  "mentalHealthSection1Desc2": "Good mental health does not mean feeling happy every moment. It means having the emotional resilience to navigate sadness, anger, stress, and joy in balanced, constructive ways.",
  "mentalHealthSection1Alert": "Mental health is just as important as physical health. The mind and body are deeply interconnected: prolonged physical illness impacts emotional health, and chronic stress directly triggers physical ailments.",
  "mentalHealthSection2Title": "Common Challenges in Youth",
  "mentalHealthSection2Intro": "Young people experience intense transitions, biological changes, and social pressures. Common challenges include:",
  "mentalHealthChallenge1": "Academic stress and burnout: Overwhelming anxiety about school grades, examinations, and future expectations.",
  "mentalHealthChallenge2": "General and social anxiety: Persistent, excessive worry about social situations, fear of judgment, or constant nervousness.",
  "mentalHealthChallenge3": "Depressive mood and sadness: Ongoing feelings of hopelessness, emptiness, loss of interest in favorite activities, and low energy.",
  "mentalHealthChallenge4": "Peer pressure and cyberbullying: Pressure to conform to unhealthy behaviors, fear of missing out (FOMO), or online harassment.",
  "mentalHealthSection3Title": "Positive Coping & Self-Care Strategies",
  "mentalHealthSection3Intro": "Building daily mental wellness practices helps protect against stress and strengthens emotional resilience:",
  "mentalHealthStrategy1": "Daily physical movement: 30 minutes of walking, jogging, dancing, or sports naturally releases endorphins that boost mood and reduce tension.",
  "mentalHealthStrategy2": "Restful sleep hygiene: Consistent 7–9 hours of sleep per night stabilizes mood, memory, and cognitive focus.",
  "mentalHealthStrategy3": "Mindful breathing and relaxation: Deep belly breathing, quiet reflection, and grounding exercises calm the nervous system when overwhelmed.",
  "mentalHealthStrategy4": "Expressive outlets: Journaling feelings, creating art, music, or prayer and meditation provide safe emotional release.",
  "mentalHealthStrategy5": "Healthy boundaries: Learning to say 'no' to overwhelming requests and limiting excessive, draining social media use.",
  "mentalHealthSection4Title": "Recognizing Warning Signs & Seeking Support",
  "mentalHealthSection4Intro": "It is important to recognize when stress or sadness has crossed into a health challenge that needs outside support:",
  "mentalHealthSign1": "Prolonged withdrawal: Isolating completely from friends, family, and social activities for weeks.",
  "mentalHealthSign2": "Extreme mood shifts: Sudden, drastic fluctuations in personality, explosive anger, or extreme hopelessness.",
  "mentalHealthSign3": "Changes in sleeping or eating: Severe insomnia, sleeping all day, or major appetite and weight changes.",
  "mentalHealthSign4": "Turning to substances: Relying on alcohol, khat, or drugs to escape difficult feelings.",
  "mentalHealthSign5": "Thoughts of self-harm: Persistent thoughts of wanting to disappear, hurting oneself, or despair.",
  "mentalHealthHelpAlert": "You never have to carry emotional pain alone. Talking to a verified peer counselor, school counselor, or calling toll-free national health lines (952 or 8335) connects you with compassionate, 100% confidential support.",
  "mentalHealthQuizTitle": "Mental Health & Wellbeing Quiz",
  "mentalHealthQ1Text": "Mental health is just as important as physical health, and experiencing emotional struggles is a medical condition, not a sign of personal weakness?",
  "mentalHealthQ1Insight": "Mental health is a fundamental component of total health. Seeking support for emotional distress is a responsible, courageous step toward wellbeing.",
  "mentalHealthQ2Text": "Which of the following is a healthy, effective way to manage everyday stress and emotional overwhelm?",
  "mentalHealthQ2OptA": "Regular physical exercise, mindful breathing, and getting 7–9 hours of sleep",
  "mentalHealthQ2OptB": "Isolating yourself in your room and avoiding talking to anyone",
  "mentalHealthQ2OptC": "Using alcohol or drugs to numb difficult emotions",
  "mentalHealthQ2OptD": "Skipping meals and staying awake all night",
  "mentalHealthQ2Insight": "Physical activity releases mood-lifting endorphins, while adequate sleep and deep breathing reset the autonomic nervous system.",
  "mentalHealthQ3Text": "Does good mental health mean you should feel happy and positive every single moment of your life?",
  "mentalHealthQ3Insight": "Good mental health involves experiencing the full range of human emotions (including sadness, frustration, and grief) and having healthy tools to process them.",
  "mentalHealthQ4Text": "If you or a friend are feeling overwhelmed by sadness or anxiety for several weeks, what is the best course of action?",
  "mentalHealthQ4OptA": "Reach out to a trusted counselor, youth mentor, or call toll-free hotlines 952 / 8335",
  "mentalHealthQ4OptB": "Keep it completely secret and hope it goes away on its own",
  "mentalHealthQ4OptC": "Criticize the friend for feeling sad",
  "mentalHealthQ4OptD": "Ignore school and stop eating",
  "mentalHealthQ4Insight": "Early conversations with peer mentors, counselors, or calling national hotlines (952 or 8335) provide confidential, life-saving support.",
